rkllm-runtime (@VERSION@) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make the service configuration a dpkg conffile. /etc/default/rkllm-server
    was a plain file, so installing a new version silently replaced it and the
    operator's RKLLM_MODEL and RKLLM_API_KEY were gone - the one thing a
    configuration file must never do. dpkg now keeps local edits on upgrade.
  * The file is still deleted when the package is removed. dpkg keeps conffiles
    on a plain `remove` and deletes them only on `purge`, so postrm unlinks it
    itself, and the package's "removal leaves nothing behind" contract is
    unchanged.
  * Document every server parameter in that file, in English and in Chinese,
    each with an optional example that is commented out. `rkllm serve` now also
    takes every option from its own RKLLM_* environment variable, so a setting
    no longer has to be spelled out as a raw argument. A value that does not
    parse is reported and ignored instead of aborting the argument parser,
    which used to show up in the journal as nothing but "status=2".
  * The text of the file no longer depends on the package version, so an upgrade
    cannot rewrite it and cannot reset a `chmod 600` applied because an API key
    lives in it. A self test and a packaging check lock both properties down.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report token usage with a language-independent estimate. The counts came
    from len(text.split()), which finds no spaces in Chinese and therefore
    reported a 15-token question as "1" - wrong by roughly an order of magnitude
    for every Chinese request, in the field clients use to budget cost and
    context. The new estimate is byte-based and identical to the one the
    prompt-size guard already used.
  * Add a self test for it. The previous usage check only verified the numbers
    were non-zero and internally consistent, and wrong values satisfied that
    too; the new one fails on any space-free input that is under-counted.
  * Document the sampling options (--top-k, --top-p, --temperature,
    --repeat-penalty) and --json in --help; they previously appeared as bare
    flags with no explanation of what they do or which way to turn them. The
    same for --model-dir and --lib under list/info/selftest, which define their
    own copies of those options.
  * Add docstrings to the public entry points (cmd_chat, cmd_run, cmd_serve,
    cmd_list, cmd_info, build_parser, main), the three request paths
    (_run_inference, do_POST, do_GET) and RealEngine.__init__, whose lifetime
    rule (one handle owns the KV cache for the whole process) was only implied
    by the surrounding code.
  * No behaviour change beyond the token estimate.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Serve requests whose system prompt does not fit the context window, instead
    of refusing them. An agent-style chat client sends tens of kilobytes of its
    own preamble - one such client sent 57 KB alongside a two-character "hello",
    which no 4096-token window can hold, so the user could not even say hello.
    When the preamble is what overflows, it is now dropped and the request is
    answered; the journal records its size. Requires the user's own text to fit.
  * The inference log now separates system and user byte counts, so it is
    immediately clear which part a request was made of. A bare "prompt 57283
    bytes" did not say whether the user pasted a document or the client attached
    an enormous preamble.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the service being killed by a large prompt. The prompt guard allowed
    eight bytes per token, so a 32 KB English prompt (roughly 8000 tokens)
    passed a 4096-token window check and the runtime overran its KV cache and
    died with SIGSEGV. The guard now assumes three bytes per token, the safe
    side for both English (about 4) and Chinese (about 3). Found by stress
    testing: a 32668-byte prompt took the whole service down.
  * Prompt and generation now share the context window: a max_tokens that does
    not fit alongside the prompt is trimmed to what remains, so a long prompt
    gets a shorter answer instead of a crash.
  * Raise the listening socket's accept queue from socketserver's default of 5
    to 256. A burst of 60 concurrent requests produced 15 client-side connection
    errors and almost no 503s, because the kernel dropped connections before
    they reached the queue logic that is meant to shed load.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Record every rejected request in the journal with its reason, and make the
    "no usable text" reply name what actually arrived (per-message role, content
    type and length). A chat UI turns our 400 into "the model is unavailable",
    so a client probing a model with an empty message - content: null, which
    several UIs send from an empty input box - was indistinguishable from a
    genuinely broken model. The journal line now says which it is.
  * The rejection counter now covers all invalid requests, not only the empty
    content case, so /health reports a truthful number.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Answer requests that carry no user turn instead of rejecting them. Clients
    probe a newly configured model with a system-only (or assistant-only)
    request, and the old "no user text found in 'messages'" reply made a
    perfectly working model look broken in the client's UI (Dify and friends
    surface the 400 as "the model is unavailable"). The last message carrying
    any text is now used as the prompt.
  * A system-only request is no longer prepended to itself, so the text reaches
    the model exactly once.
  * Fix an AttributeError (which surfaced as HTTP 500) when a client sent the
    system prompt in the multimodal content shape - a list of parts - instead of
    a plain string.
  * Log the shapes of the messages whenever a request is rejected for lack of
    usable text, so the journal alone shows what the client sent.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the service failing with exit code 2 and nothing useful in the journal.
    systemd expands an unset variable into an EMPTY ARGUMENT, so the bare
    ${RKLLM_PLATFORM_ARG} / ${RKLLM_EXTRA_ARGS} in ExecStart passed two stray
    empty arguments to rkllm, which argparse rejected as unrecognised
    positionals. ExecStart is now wrapped in /bin/sh, where an unquoted empty
    variable disappears instead of becoming an empty argument.
  * rkllm itself now ignores empty arguments and writes the "no command given"
    message to stderr, so the reason reaches the journal.
  * The self test no longer aborts silently when a check triggers sys.exit();
    it reports the abort and still prints the summary.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The systemd unit no longer guards on ConditionPathExists=/dev/rknpu. Drivers
    from 0.9.x onwards register the NPU as a DRM render node and do NOT create
    that path, so the guard silently skipped the unit on perfectly working
    boards - "skipped, unmet condition check" - and the service never started.
    Whether an NPU is usable is decided by the runtime at startup, and
    StartLimitBurst keeps a board without one from restarting forever.
  * `rkllm info` and `rkllm selftest` now look for the driver through several
    signals instead of one path: /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version, a DRM render
    node whose driver link points at rknpu, the loaded kernel module, and the
    legacy /dev/rknpu device node.
  * Documentation, README.Debian and both manual pages corrected: they no
    longer claim the runtime needs /dev/rknpu.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop calling rkllm_set_chat_template to install a system prompt. That call
    disables the model's built-in chat template parsing - the runtime warns
    about this - after which the model receives bare text without any of its
    special tokens: it rambles, answers as if continuing a document, and often
    never emits EOS, which the user sees as a request that hangs. The setting
    persists for the whole handle, so a single system message poisoned every
    later request until the service was restarted.
  * System text (both --system and a request-level system message) is now
    prepended to the prompt, which leaves the built-in template intact.
  * New self test check `system message reaches the model` locks this down.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Report prompt/completion token counts in the response usage block. They were
    always zero, so chat clients displayed "0 tokens" for every answer.
  * The counts remain whitespace estimates: no tokeniser is available in this
    script, which the manual page and the README say explicitly.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a segmentation fault that killed the API server: rkllm_set_chat_template
    was called with NULL for the prompt prefix and postfix, which the runtime
    dereferences unconditionally. Any client that sends a system message (which
    is every chat UI) crashed the process.
  * Refuse prompts far larger than the model's context window with HTTP 400
    instead of letting the runtime run off the end of its KV cache.
  * Log the request size before inference starts, so the last line before a
    crash identifies the offending request.
  * Accept the API paths with and without the /v1 prefix, and explain the
    expected base URL in 404 responses and in `rkllm serve --help`.
  * /health now reports the model name, queue length and uptime.
  * Require the API key through $RKLLM_API_KEY or --api-key, compared in
    constant time, and describe it in the manual page.
  * Add English and simplified Chinese manual pages (/usr/share/man/man1 and
    /usr/share/man/zh_CN/man1).
  * Add rkllm-server.service and /etc/default/rkllm-server; installed but never
    enabled or started automatically. postinst restarts a service that was
    already running, prerm stops it, postrm drops any enable symlink.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@

rkllm-runtime (1.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian packaging built from the upstream rknn-llm release tarball.
  * Files relocated from the upstream SDK tree to FHS/multiarch paths.
  * Example sources installed read-only; see /usr/share/doc/@PACKAGE@/README.Debian.
  * New: /usr/bin/rkllm, a front end for local chat, one-shot inference and an
    OpenAI-compatible API server. Standard library only: no compilation step on
    the board and no pip packages.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@
